\begin_layout Standard
An asset is a divisible claim to a financial return in the future.
 Suppose that there are two assets, a safe asset with a return of 1 dollar
 per dollar invested and a risky asset with a random return of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 dollars per dollar invested.
 The random return 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 has the following probability distribution: 
\begin_inset Formula $z=0$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z=1$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z=2$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\alpha-\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\series bold
risk neutral
\series default
 individual, who we refer to as agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, has aninitial wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 to invest in these two assets.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the part of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A\mbox{'s}$
\end_inset

 wealth invested in the risky asset.
 The amount invested in the safe asset is 
\begin_inset Formula $w-x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for any realtization the random return 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A\mbox{'s}$
\end_inset

 portfolio 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,w-x)$
\end_inset

 pays 
\begin_inset Formula $xz+w-x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Characterize agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A\mbox{'s}$
\end_inset

 optimal portfolio, i.e., find 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 When does 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 invest in the risky asset? Interpret.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset

We assume that agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is an expected utility maximizer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 denote the c.d.f of the random return 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 for the risky asset, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 denote the c.d.f of the certain return for the safe asset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the results of problem 3, the risk neutral preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is the one that 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{F,G\}\subseteq\Delta(Z),F\succsim G\iff\mu_{F}\geq\mu_{G}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{F}=(\alpha(0)+\beta(1)+(1-\alpha-\beta)2)=2-2\alpha-\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{G}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $F\succsim G\iff2-2\alpha-\beta\geq1\iff2\alpha-\beta\leq1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From problem 3, since agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is risk neutral, her index utility function is linear.
 So, to maximize her expected utility, she will either invest all of her
 wealth, 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, in the risky asset, or none of it.
 Thus, we can characterize 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x=\begin{cases}
w & \mbox{if}\ 2\alpha-\beta<1\\
\{0,w\} & \mbox{if}\ 2\alpha-\beta=1\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ 2\alpha-\beta>1
\end{cases}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 will invest in the risky asset if its expected return, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{F}$
\end_inset

, is greater than the certain return of the safe asset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Suppose now that agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 cannot directly invest in these two assets.
 Instead, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 can invest through a financial manager who gets a percentage of the agent's
 return.
 Suppose for simplicity that agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 would receive a return of zero if she does not invest through a financial
 manager.
 There are two financial managers: 
\begin_inset Formula $M1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M2$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that the initial wealth of 
\begin_inset Formula $M1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M2$
\end_inset

 is zero and that each gets a portion 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 of the corresponding agents' returns.
 For instance, if agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 dollars to 
\begin_inset Formula $M1$
\end_inset

 and this finaancial manager invests 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 in the risky asset and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}-x_{1}$
\end_inset

 in the safe asset, then for each realization of the random return, the
 manager gets 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(x_{1}z+y_{1}-x_{1})$
\end_inset

 and agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 gets 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\varphi)(x_{1}z+y_{1}-x_{1}).$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(i) Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $M1$
\end_inset

 is an expected utility maximizer with vNM utility index: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(x)\equiv\sqrt{x}$
\end_inset

.
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 dollars to 
\begin_inset Formula $M1$
\end_inset

, what is the manager's optimal portfolio? That is, calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 When does 
\begin_inset Formula $M1$
\end_inset

 invest in the risky asset?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \max_{0\le x_{1}\leq y_{1}}{\displaystyle \int\sqrt{\varphi(x_{1}z+y_{1}-x_{1})}\ dF}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $={\displaystyle \max_{0\le x_{1}\leq y_{1}}}{\displaystyle \alpha\sqrt{\varphi(y_{1}-x_{1})}+\beta\sqrt{\varphi(x_{1}+y_{1}-x_{1})}+(1-\alpha-\beta)\sqrt{\varphi(2x_{1}+y_{1}-x_{1})}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FOC of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{F}u_{1}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{F}u_{1}}{\partial x}=-\varphi\frac{\alpha}{2\sqrt{\varphi(y_{1}-x_{1})}}+\varphi\frac{(1-\alpha-\beta)}{2\sqrt{\varphi(x_{1}+y_{1})}}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\iff\frac{\alpha}{2\sqrt{\varphi(y_{1}-x_{1})}}=\frac{(1-\alpha-\beta)}{2\sqrt{\varphi(x_{1}+y_{1})}}$
\end_inset


